---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Milka"
  eyebrow="Senj, 9 October 1886 — 7 September 1971"
  dek="Hedviga's mother is the best-documented person in this archive: a birth entry, two marriage entries, a gravestone and a factory. Her life can be told almost entirely from records, and it is not the life the family tree describes.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The tree carries <strong>Milka Lucia Papić</strong> as the wife of Ljubomir
    Blažević and the mother of seven. The registers carry a woman who was
    <strong>married twice</strong>, <strong>widowed at twenty-eight</strong>, and spent
    <strong>more than forty years</strong> in the same tobacco factory — going in as a floor worker
    and coming out supervising it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Senj baptisms, page 122, entry 96</div>
    <h2>Born 9 October 1886</h2>
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/line/4505839-milka-lucia-birth-1886.jpg")}
           alt="Senj baptismal register 1886, page 122, entry 96 — Milka Lucia Papić" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Senj, Roman Catholic baptisms, <em>Str. 122</em>, entry 96. Her entry is the
      second from the bottom of the left-hand page.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <tr><th>Name</th><td><strong>Milka Lucia</strong> — <em>zakonita</em>, legitimate</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Born / baptised</th><td><strong>9 October 1886</strong>, baptised
        <strong>17 October</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Father</th><td><a href={u("/people/josip-anton-papic/")}><strong>Josip
        Papić</strong></a>, <strong><em>trhonoša</em></strong> — a load-carrier</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Mother</th><td><a href={u("/people/antonija-tonka-perpic-prpic/")}><strong>Tonka
        <em>rođ.</em> Prpić</strong></a></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Godparents</th><td><strong>Josip Glavičić</strong>, also a <em>trhonoša</em>, and
        <strong>Ivka Filipović</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Baptised by</th><td>the chaplain (<em>kapelan</em>) at Senj</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Later annotation</th><td>The margin was written up again when she died — the register
        records the death entry and the date <strong>7.9.1971</strong>, endorsed at Senj
        <strong>30.9.1971</strong>.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note"><strong>A small correction.</strong> The family tree gives her baptism as
    <strong>19 October</strong>. The register says <strong>17</strong>. The archive follows the
    register.</div>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Both her father and her godfather are <em>trhonoše</em> — men who
    carried other people's goods on their backs for a fee. That is not a coincidence; it is a
    social world. In a port town whose port had died, <a href={u("/senj/")}>the porters knew the
    porters</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Senj marriages, page 294, entry 30</div>
    <h2>First marriage, 4 November 1907</h2>
    <p class="lede">Aged twenty-one, she married <a href={u("/people/zvonimir-matei-vukelic/")}>
    <strong>Zvonimir M. Vukelić</strong></a> — and nothing in the family tree's account of her had
    ever mentioned it.</p>
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/line/4502611-zvonomir-and-milka-marriage.jpg")}
           alt="Senj marriage register 1907, page 294, entry 30" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Senj marriages, page 294, entry 30, boxed in red on the scan as held in the tree.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <tr><th>Date</th><td><strong>4 <em>studenoga</em> (November) 1907</strong>, Senj</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Groom</th><td><strong>Zvonimir M. Vukelić</strong>,
        <em>gostioničarski pomoćnik</em> — <strong>innkeeper's assistant</strong>. Born
        <strong>13 January 1883</strong>. Bachelor.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Bride</th><td><strong>Milka Papić</strong>,
        <strong><em>radnica u tvornici duhana</em></strong> — a worker in the tobacco factory. Born
        9 October 1886. Unmarried.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>His parents</th><td><strong>Petar Vukelić</strong>, <em>gostioničar</em> — innkeeper,
        marked deceased — and <strong>Vica</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Her parents</th><td><strong>Joso Papić, <em>gostioničar</em></strong> and
        <strong>Tonka <em>rođ.</em> Prpić</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Witnesses</th><td><strong>Kolomán <em>pl.</em> Vukelić</strong>, a broker at the
        tobacco factory, and <strong>Franjo Olivieri</strong>, merchant</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Banns</th><td>Married by <strong>episcopal dispensation</strong>, with the banns read
        once — on <strong>3 November</strong>, the day before the wedding — instead of the usual
        three times</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note"><strong>Her father has changed trade.</strong> In 1886 Josip Papić is a
    <em>trhonoša</em>. By 1907 the register calls him <strong><em>gostioničar</em></strong> — an
    innkeeper. The tree lists his occupations as “<em>Trhonosa, bremenar, gostionicar</em>”, and the
    two registers show that string is a <strong>sequence</strong>: the porter kept the money and
    bought a bar. <a href={u("/papic/")}>The Papići →</a></div>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem">The one-day dispensation is worth noticing without
    over-reading. Her daughter <strong>Miroslava</strong> was born in <strong>1907</strong>. A
    hurried wedding and a child in the same year is a familiar shape, but the register does not date
    the birth and this archive does not claim to know which came first.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Two children, and a widow at twenty-eight</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The Vukelić children</h3>
        <p><a href={u("/people/miroslava-mica-nana-vukelic/")}><strong>Miroslava “Mica” /
        “Nana” Vukelić</strong></a> (1907–1993), who married <strong>Rahamin Levi</strong>
        (1904–1984) of <strong>Pirot</strong>; and
        <a href={u("/people/josip-vukelic/")}><strong>Josip Vukelić</strong></a> (1913–2004),
        born at Senj.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">They are <strong>Hedviga's half-brother and half-sister</strong>,
        and they were in the tree all along — invisible to the first build of this archive only
        because a sweep of somebody's <em>ancestors</em> never walks sideways.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>1915</h3>
        <p>Zvonimir Vukelić <strong>died in 1915</strong>, aged thirty-two, in the middle of the
        First World War. Milka was <strong>twenty-eight</strong>, with a girl of eight and a boy of
        two.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">Nothing in this archive says how he died, and 1915 is a year in
        which a man of thirty-two might die many ways.
        <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Senj marriages, page 366, entry 41</div>
    <h2>Second marriage, 8 December 1920</h2>
    <p class="lede">Thirteen years after the first wedding and five years after the death, she
    married <a href={u("/people/ljubomir-blazevic/")}><strong>Ljubomir Blažević</strong></a>, a
    sailor. The register enters her under her married name:
    <strong>Milka Vukelić, <em>udova</em></strong> — widow.</p>
    <p>She is still <strong><em>radnica u tvornici duhana</em></strong>. Thirteen years on from her
    first marriage entry, the same job, the same words.
    <a href={u("/marriage-1920/")}>The full entry →</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Seven children followed, of whom
    <a href={u("/hedviga/")}><strong>Hedviga</strong></a> was the fourth, born 3 August 1926 — and
    <a href={u("/oto/")}>Oto</a>, who did not come home from the war.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The factory, over a lifetime</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>1907, aged 21</th><td><em>Radnica u tvornici duhana</em> — <strong>worker</strong>,
        per the marriage register</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1920, aged 34</th><td><em>Radnica u tvornici duhana</em> — still a
        <strong>worker</strong>, per the marriage register</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Later</th><td><strong>Supervisor of workers</strong> at the cigarette factory, per the
        family tree — at <em class="mono">Ul. Stjepana Radića 4</em>, “now a textile
        factory”</td></tr>
      <tr><th>And beside her</th><td><strong>Anka Blažević</strong> (1893–1953), her husband's
        sister, a <em>radnica</em> in the same factory</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">This is the only career progression documented anywhere in this
    archive, and it belongs to a woman. In a town where the men are recorded as day labourers,
    porters and sailors, <strong>the steady wage was hers</strong>.
    <a href={u("/senj/")}>Why there was no other work →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>7 September 1971</h2>
    <p>She died at Senj, aged eighty-four, and is on the family stone as
    <strong>MILKA BLAŽEVIĆ <em>rod.</em> PAPIĆ ✳ 1886 † 1971</strong> — under her
    <em>maiden</em> name, not the Vukelić name she carried into her second wedding, and beside the
    son whose body was never found. <a href={u("/grave/")}>The grave →</a></p>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>What is still missing.</strong> Zvonimir
    Vukelić's death entry for 1915, which would say how she was widowed; Miroslava's 1907 birth
    entry, which would fix the order of wedding and child; and any record of what Milka did between
    1915 and 1920, alone with two children in a town with no work.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
